      "quickCheck": {
        "prompt": "A fifth-grade student has $10 of allowance. She can buy a book for $8 or save the money toward a $30 game. She buys the book. Using the concept of opportunity cost, what did she give up, and how should a teacher use this example in a financial literacy lesson?",
        "answer": "The opportunity cost of buying the book is the next-best alternative she gave up: the progress she could have made toward saving for the $30 game with that money. A teacher can use this scenario to show that every financial choice has a trade-off, and that opportunity cost is the value of what you choose not to do, not the price you pay. This moves students beyond simply labeling needs versus wants and toward the reasoning behind budgeting decisions.",
        "hint": "Opportunity cost is not the price paid; it is the value of the best alternative given up. What did the student move farther away from by spending the money?"
      },
